vaulthalla (1.22.1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * S3: `Controller::uploadLargeObject` (file and buffer overloads) throws
    after aborting a multipart upload whose part failed. It used to return
    normally, so `CloudEngine` uploads, backlog replays and recovery restores
    of objects >= 5 MiB treated the aborted upload as stored. Regression test
    `S3VersionedFake.AFailedMultipartPartAbortsAndThrowsForBuffersAndFiles`
    runs the real driver against the versioned fake.
  * S3/SQS/STS: the upstream control plane (bucket administration, event
    notifications, `provider::admin::detail::send`) and the object requests
    built by `Controller::signObjectRequest` (the full bucket LIST of
    `listObjects`, ListObjectVersions, HEAD/GET/COPY with `versionId`) sign
    with `vl::sigv4::sign` from vl-sigv4 (Valkyrian Labs, MIT) instead of the
    in-tree `storage::s3::sigv4::sign`. Same bytes on the wire:
    `SigV4Goldens.VlSigv4MatchesUpstreamGoldens` and
    `VlSigv4SendsWhatTheUpstreamSignerSent` hold it to every upstream golden
    record. The in-tree signer stays as the reference until a release is
    verified.
  * SES: `SesProvider::send` (SES v2 SendEmail) signs with `vl::sigv4::sign`
    (service `ses`) instead of `email::providers::detail::signAwsV4`. Same
    request on the wire: `SigV4Goldens.VlSigv4MatchesSesGoldens`,
    `VlSigv4SendsWhatTheSesSignerSent` and `SesProviderSendSignsLikeTheGoldens`
    (URL, header lines, Authorization). The old signer, kept as the goldens'
    reference until a release is verified, deviated from SigV4 for inputs
    SendEmail never has (unsorted query, bare flag parameters, untrimmed header
    values, single-encoded non-S3 paths); `VlSigv4FixesTheSesSignersDeviations`
    holds vl-sigv4 to the spec there.
  * S3 data path: object PUT (buffer, file, conditional), GET (buffer, file,
    streamed), HEAD, copy-in-place metadata updates, DELETE,
    `listObjectKeysPage`, multipart initiate/part/complete/abort, the
    credential probe and `isBucketEmpty` sign with `curl::signDataPathRequest`
    (behind `curl::buildAuthorizationHeader`), built on vl-sigv4's primitives,
    instead of the in-tree legacy signer. Callers still build the URL, the
    header lines and the payload hash (`UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD` or the body's
    SHA-256, per operation, unchanged), so the request is the same:
    `SigV4Goldens.VlSigv4MatchesLegacyGoldens` and
    `VlSigv4SendsWhatTheLegacySignerSent` hold it to 23 of the 24 legacy
    records, and `S3DataPathWire.*` sends every request shape through real curl
    to a capture server and checks that vl-sigv4's verifier accepts it and that
    the old signer computes the same Authorization for it.
    - One time source: `buildHeaderMap` no longer sets `x-amz-date`; the signer
      sets it from the instant it signs at and takes the credential-scope date
      from the same instant. The old signer read the scope date from a second
      clock read (`getDate()`), so a request stamped just before UTC midnight
      and signed just after carried two dates and was refused
      (`VlSigv4DataPathDatesComeFromOneInstant`).
    - Header values are signed trimmed with inner whitespace collapsed, as
      SigV4 says; the old signer signed them verbatim, which fails at S3 for
      values with extra whitespace. No data-path header carries any (every
      value is generated or an ETag read back trimmed), so no request changes;
      `VlSigv4FixesTheLegacySignersDeviations`.
    - `curl::buildAuthorizationTrace` (the old signer) has no production
      callers and stays as the goldens' reference until a release is verified.
  * SES: an `email.ses.endpoint` with a path prefix (an SES proxy at
    `https://host/aws`) is signed for the path it is sent to.
    `SesProvider::send` used to send to `<endpoint>/v2/email/outbound-emails`
    while signing `/v2/email/outbound-emails`, so every send through such an
    endpoint failed with SignatureDoesNotMatch. The endpoint is split into
    origin, Host and prefix, the prefix is signed with the SES path, and the
    URL is built from what was signed. Endpoints without a path are unchanged.
    Regression test `SesProviderTest.EndpointPathPrefixIsSentAndSigned`
    verifies the sent request with vl-sigv4's verifier.
  * Build: new dependency vl-sigv4 (0.2.1), a static library built from the
    release tarball pinned in `subprojects/vl-sigv4.wrap` (sha256 checked by
    meson) and committed in `subprojects/packagecache/`, so the package build
    stays offline (`--wrap-mode=nodownload`). Needs only libcrypto >= 3.0,
    already a build dependency; installs nothing into the package.
  * S3 gateway: SigV4 verification moves to vl-sigv4 (`vl::sigv4::verify` with
    a `SecretLookup`): `protocols::s3::verifySignedRequest` parses, runs every
    secret-free check (date, skew or presign window, signed headers, payload
    hash), then looks the credential up, then compares the signature in
    constant time. `Router::route` builds the input with
    `protocols::s3::verificationInput` (Beast adapter). The in-tree
    `protocols/s3/SigV4` has no production callers and stays as the goldens'
    reference until a release is verified.
    - Same acceptance for real clients: 126 requests captured from aws-cli
      2.38, boto3 1.34, rclone 1.69 and mc 2025-08 over HTTP and HTTPS
      (`core/test-assets/sigv4/client-requests.jsonl`,
      `tools/dev/sigv4-capture/`) verify as before, and
      `SigV4Goldens.VlSigv4VerifierMatchesGatewayGoldens` rebuilds every S3
      gateway record.
    - Error codes follow AWS (`vl::sigv4::awsErrorCode`; statuses in
      `sigv4Error`): RequestTimeTooSkewed (403), InvalidAccessKeyId (403, was
      AccessDenied), AuthorizationHeaderMalformed /
      AuthorizationQueryParametersError (400), XAmzContentSHA256Mismatch (400),
      AccessDenied (403) for a missing Authorization, missing or invalid date
      or an expired presigned URL, NotImplemented (501) for signed-chunk
      payloads, InvalidArgument (400) for header plus presigned query.
      Everything else was SignatureDoesNotMatch (403); a wrong signature and a
      missing signed header still are, and session tokens are still
      InvalidToken (400).
    - Newly refused (AWS refuses them too): host not signed, credential scope
      date != x-amz-date date, X-Amz-Expires=0, Authorization header plus
      presigned query, malformed x-amz-date, credential scope or SignedHeaders,
      Authorization parts without '='. Newly accepted (spec-valid, refused
      before): repeated signed headers combined with ',', an X-Amz-Signature
      query parameter on a header-signed request, upper-case hex in
      x-amz-content-sha256.
    - The credential lookup (a DB query and the secret's decryption) now runs
      after the date and payload checks, not before them.
  * S3 gateway: signed streaming uploads (`STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256-PAYLOAD`
    and `-TRAILER`: mc and the AWS Java SDK over plain HTTP) are accepted. They
    were `NotImplemented` (501) before.
    - `Session` spools a streamed PUT as before. It now hands aws-chunked
      bodies to `Router::route` still encoded
      (`BodyPayload::aws_chunked_decode_to`) when Content-Encoding says
      `aws-chunked` or `x-amz-content-sha256` is any `STREAMING-*` mode. mc
      sends signed chunks without Content-Encoding.
    - `Router::route` decodes the body with `decodeAwsChunkedBody`
      (`protocols/s3/RequestBody.cpp`, vl-sigv4's `AwsChunkedDecoder`).
      Unsigned framing is decoded before authentication, as before. Signed
      chunks are decoded after it, through the `vl::sigv4::ChunkVerifier` that
      `Authenticator::authenticate(input, &chain)` /
      `verifySignedRequest(..., &chain)` returns once the seed signature
      verified.
    - `routeAuthenticated` runs only after the final chunk (and the trailer
      signature) verified. A tampered, reordered, dropped or missing chunk
      stores nothing and leaves an existing object unchanged.
    - The Session's own aws-chunked decoder (`decodeAwsChunkedBody` in
      `Session.cpp`) is gone. Moving to the library parser changes some
      answers:
    - Framing errors were a `std::runtime_error` that closed the connection
      without a response. They are now S3 errors: `IncompleteBody` (400) for a
      truncated body, a missing final chunk or an
      `x-amz-decoded-content-length` mismatch; `InvalidRequest` (400) for
      malformed framing, a chunk over 16 MiB or trailers that differ from
      `x-amz-trailer`; `SignatureDoesNotMatch` (403) for a chunk or trailer
      signature; `InvalidArgument` for a non-numeric
      `x-amz-decoded-content-length`.
    - Stricter, as AWS and MinIO are: chunk sizes are 1 to 16 hex digits (no
      sign, whitespace or `0x`), chunk headers end in CRLF, header and trailer
      lines are at most 4 KiB, trailer lines need a `name:` token, and no bytes
      may follow the end of the body. The trailer layouts of the AWS SDKs and
      of MinIO's Go client are both accepted.
    - Signed chunks outside a streamed PUT, and SigV4a streaming, still answer
      `NotImplemented`. New `sigv4StatusFor` rows: `ChunkSignatureMismatch`
      403; `MalformedChunkedEncoding`, `ChunkTooLarge`, `IncompleteBody`,
      `DecodedLengthMismatch` 400.
    - Tests:
    - `S3GatewayStreaming.*` covers mc- and SDK-shaped uploads, trailers and
      every failure class.
    - `S3GatewayClientCaptures` replays mc's real uploads over plain HTTP (one
      chunk, three chunks, and `mc cp --checksum` unsigned trailers), each
      verified and decoded at its signing time. Captures come from the new
      `tools/dev/sigv4-capture/capture_mc_streaming.sh`, and a flipped byte in
      a signed body fails.
    - `S3GatewayDbTest.SignedStreamingUploadsStoreOnlyFullyVerifiedBodies` runs
      end to end over a socket. Tampered, reordered, dropped and unfinished
      bodies store nothing, and a tampered overwrite keeps the old object.
    - `S3GatewayDbTest.RealMcUploadsOverPlainHttpWhenMcIsInstalled` runs a real
      mc against the running gateway and skips where mc is not installed.
  * Tests: SigV4 characterization goldens
    (`core/tests/unit/test_sigv4_goldens.cpp`, `core/test-assets/sigv4/`) pin
    the canonical requests and signatures of the upstream, legacy data-path,
    SES and gateway signers ahead of their consolidation. Two test seams, no
    behaviour change: `curl::buildAuthorizationTrace` (the legacy signer with
    the scope date given) and `email::providers::detail::signAwsV4`
    (SesProvider's signer, now in `SesSigning.hpp`).
  * Release CI: the macOS `.pkg` and its `.sha256` publish to Apthalla's raw
    repository `valkyrian-raw` (VPS loopback port 8090; `pkg.vaulthalla.sh`
    serves it) instead of Nexus. `tools/macos/pkg/publish-nexus.sh` is now
    `publish-pkg.sh` (`PKG_REPO_URL`, uploads carry `X-Checksum-SHA256`;
    immutable, read back as before) and the release job is `macos-publish-pkg`.
    README.Debian's needrestart example names a placeholder `example.service`
    instead of `nexus.service`.
  * Offline startup (#316): `ProtocolService` no longer downloads password
    lists before starting the ws/http listeners (a failed download threw out of
    `runLoop` and the listeners never started without internet). The lists ship
    as `/usr/share/vaulthalla/password-lists/{dictionary,common-passwords}.txt`
    (SecLists NCSC top 100k, MIT; SCOWL size 50 dictionary, SCOWL licence;
    provenance in `deploy/password-lists/README.md`) and load once per process
    from local files, never throwing. The unlicensed `dolph/dictionary` list is
    gone.
    - `Strength::checkPwnedPassword` returns `NotFound`/`Found`/`Unavailable`,
      with a 3 s connect and 5 s total timeout, a 4 MiB response cap and no
      redirects; `Unavailable` no longer blocks a password change.
    - Lists swap in under a mutex (readers take snapshots). Tests:
      `OfflineStartup.*` (listeners up with no list files and zero outbound
      requests; refused, blackholed, unresolvable and HTTP-error breach
      lookups).
  * Protocols: `ProtocolService` and the device listener keep their io_context
    for the life of the process while any `ws::Session` object is alive (new
    `ws::Session::liveCount()`, a `SessionLifetimes` token released after the
    socket). ws sessions stay cached in the session manager across a listener
    restart; freeing the context made their later destruction a use-after-free
    (intermittent SIGSEGV, found by `OfflineStartup.ListenersStart…` under
    `MALLOC_PERTURB_`: 11/40 runs before, 0/80 after).
  * Crypto (#318): `KeyProvider` generates new master secrets with libsodium's
    `randombytes_buf` (`KeyProvider::generateMasterSecret`); they came from
    `std::random_device`, one call per byte. Already-sealed secrets are
    untouched (no reseal, no rotation). `fs::ops::generate_random_suffix` (the
    names of plaintext working copies, staging and seal temporaries) uses
    `randombytes_uniform` instead of a `std::mt19937`. Contract
    `tools/contracts/test_crypto_rng_contract.py` keeps `<random>` engines out
    of `core/`; tests `MasterSecretRng.*`.
  * Auth config (#320): `convert<AuthConfig>` reads
    `access_token_expiry_minutes`, the shipped and documented key; it read only
    `token_expiry_minutes`. The old key stays a reported alias
    (`Registry::deprecations`), and the shipped key wins when both are set.
    Encode writes the shipped key. The refresh cookie's `Max-Age` follows
    `refresh_token_expiry_days` (`protocols/ws/RefreshCookie.hpp`); it was
    hard-coded to 604800. Tests:
    `ConfigSettingsTest.AccessTokenLifetimeHonoursTheShippedKey`,
    `ConfigSettingsSerialization.AuthWritesTheShippedKey`,
    `RefreshCookie.MaxAgeFollowsTheConfiguredLifetime`.
  * Auth (#319): idle sign-out for web-console sessions. Migration 116 adds
    `refresh_tokens.last_activity_at` (server `NOW()` at sign-in and on the new
    ws command `auth.activity`, written at most every 30 s; background traffic
    never touches it). `auth/session/Idle.hpp` holds the policy: human web
    sessions only (not device-listener or share sessions),
    `idle_timeout_minutes` (default 30, `0` = off, the 5-minute minimum is
    gone). Enforced in `session::Manager::validate`, `renewAccessToken`
    (`auth.refresh`), `validateRawRefreshToken` (HTTP lanes),
    `Validator::rehydrateFromStoredRefreshToken` and the lifecycle sweep
    (`SweepVerdict::IdleTimeout`, frame `session_idle_timeout`). Expiry revokes
    the refresh token (`hardInvalidate`), so the cookie can't sign back in. The
    decision re-reads the stored row first, so a stale copy in one tab never
    signs out an active sign-in. Console: `lib/activity.ts` reports pointer,
    keyboard, wheel, scroll and touch input at most once a minute; the session
    gate re-checks the session on reconnect. Tests: `IdleLogoff.*`,
    `UserParityTest.{IdleSessionsAreSignedOutAndCannotSignBackInSilently,APageLoadRefreshDoesNotRescueAnIdleSession,AnotherTabsActivityKeepsTheSharedSignInAlive,IdleSignOutCanBeTurnedOff}`,
    `web/src/lib/activity.test.ts`, `web/tests/e2e/idle-logoff.spec.ts`
    (offline scripted, plus a live variant).
  * Sharing (#322): the share-token pepper is sealed under the host master key
    (`crypto::secrets::Manager`, key `share.token.pepper.sealed.v1`). The
    plaintext `share.token.pepper.v1` row from earlier releases is converted on
    first use: the same value is sealed, read back and compared, then the
    plaintext row is deleted (resumes after an interruption; idempotent). Fails
    closed, never regenerates, when the sealed copy can't be unsealed or
    disagrees with a leftover plaintext copy. New
    `db::query::crypto::Secret::deleteSecret` (`delete_internal_secret`).
    Downgrade below this release invalidates share links. Tests:
    `SharePepperSealing.*` (DB-backed).
  * Sharing (#317): email-challenge codes are delivered.
    `share::Manager::startEmailChallenge` sends the code through
    `ChallengeCodeSender` (`share/CodeDelivery.{hpp,cpp}`; default: the
    configured `email::Provider`, idempotency key `share-challenge-<id>`), only
    when the address is a listed recipient; other addresses get the same reply
    and no email (audit `share_email_recipient_not_listed`). Send failures are
    logged and audited (`share_email_delivery_failed`), never reported. Without
    email delivery, `createLink`, an `updateLink` into `email_validated`, and
    `startEmailChallenge` throw `ops::Refused{"share_email_unavailable"}`. The
    email names no file, link or sharer. Tests:
    `ShareManagerTest.EmailChallengeSendsTheCodeToTheListedRecipientOnly`,
    `EmailValidatedLinksNeedEmailDelivery`, `AFailedSendIsAuditedNotReported`,
    `ShareCodeEmail.*`, and the ws challenge test now asserts delivery.
  * Auth (#321): `auth::Manager::loginUser` refuses every failed sign-in with
    the same message (`Manager::kLoginFailed`) and logs the reason (unknown
    account, wrong password, deactivated, system-only) at warn. Unknown names
    are verified against a once-built Argon2id hash of a random secret, and the
    password is verified before the account state is checked, so each refusal
    costs one Argon2id verify. Test:
    `UserParityTest.RefusedSignInsLookTheSame`.
  * Hash-chained, append-only audit trail in the database (migration 117:
    audit_event, audit_head, audit_checkpoint), fed by the audit logger; signed
    chain heads; retention purge with checkpoints (#323).
  * New ws commands audit.list, audit.verify, audit.export and CLI vh audit
    list|verify|export (admin.audits.view); console page /audit.
  * Sign-in, sign-out, password, role, group, override, S3 credential and
    console file mutation events;
    auditing.trail.{retention_days,head_interval_minutes,syslog,syslog_facility}.
  * TLS by default (#324): `vh setup nginx` without a certificate mode renders
    HTTPS with a local self-signed certificate
    (`/etc/vaulthalla/tls/local.{crt,key}`; ECDSA P-256, 825 days; SAN: host
    names, `localhost`, loopback and the host's addresses) and a 308 redirect;
    new `vh setup local-tls [--force]` creates or renews it (postinst runs it
    on install and upgrade). `--plain-http` keeps HTTP. `--certbot` passes
    `--hsts`; the Cloudflare DNS-01 site sends HSTS. The packaged site
    (`deploy/nginx/vaulthalla.conf`) is HTTPS; postinst replaces an installed
    site byte-identical to any earlier packaged plain-HTTP template and warns
    about other plain-HTTP sites. Its verification probe follows the redirect
    to `https://127.0.0.1/`.
  * Security headers from nginx (all managed sites) and from the console itself
    (`next.config.ts` `headers()`): nosniff, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`,
    `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, a restrictive
    `Permissions-Policy`.
  * ws handshake refuses a browser `Origin` whose host differs from
    `Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host` and isn't in the new
    `websocket_server.allowed_origins` (403 before hydration;
    `ws/OriginPolicy.hpp`).
  * Listener host defaults are `127.0.0.1` (ws, HTTP, S3 gateway: compiled,
    YAML and JSON decoders, lifecycle projection); the shipped
    `s3_gateway.host` is `127.0.0.1`.
  * Tests: `WsOrigin.*`, `ListenerDefaults.*`, lifecycle
    `HttpsByDefaultTests`/`LocalCertificateTests` (real openssl),
    `PostinstNginxTlsUpgradeTests`.
  * KeyProvider::initGuarded: vault_master, ak_master and s3gw_master are never
    regenerated while records wrapped by them exist (MasterSecretLost) (#326).
  * macOS sealer fails closed without a Secure Enclave unless
    secrets.allow_development_file_backend or -Ddarwin_sealer=file; backend
    "unavailable".
  * Health deps.secrets_posture (hardware|software|development|unknown);
    secrets.require_hardware refuses non-hardware sealing.
  * Log lines name files by entry id or an opaque per-run tag;
    logging.file_paths (ids|full, default ids) controls it (#327).
  * Console HTTP lanes address files by entry=<id>; ids follow the same RBAC,
    share scope and no-existence-oracle rules as paths.
  * Share links put the token in the URL fragment (/share#<token>);
    /share/<token> links redirect.
  * nginx sites log through a redacting log_format (share tokens, query
    strings, no Referer).
  * FUSE working copies (now `<backing>/.fuse-working`), private HTTP upload
    staging (`.upload-staging`) and `vh fs put` staging (`.cli-staging`) are
    block-encrypted at rest (XChaCha20-Poly1305 per 64 KiB block, fresh nonce
    per write, AAD bound to the file and block) under a random per-process key
    held only in locked memory (#328).
  * A working copy whose seal fails stays registered as unsaved and is retried
    every 60 s, on the next flush/fsync/release and at FUSE stop; a file
    deleted meanwhile is restored as a new file. Unsaved copies still failing
    at shutdown, or left by a crash, are deleted with an error naming how many
    were lost.
  * The startup sweep deletes everything under `.fuse-working`; plaintext an
    older version left in `.fuse-plaintext/unsaved/` is kept with a warning and
    the rest of `.fuse-plaintext` is removed.
  * `fs::NewFileContext::source_path` is replaced by `source`
    (`fs::PlaintextSource`); `EncryptionManager` gains `encryptToFile` and
    `decryptFileTo` (sink), and `decryptFileToFile` is gone, so nothing
    decrypts to a plaintext file.
  * S3 gateway request bodies are staged as `crypto::ScratchFile` ciphertext
    (process key) in `<backing>/.s3-request-body/` instead of plaintext in
    `<backing>/s3-request-body/`; both are cleared at startup (#345).
  * S3 multipart parts are sealed under the vault key (AES-256-GCM, fresh IV
    per write, AAD bound to vault, upload id, part number and size;
    `protocols/s3/PartCipher`). Migration 118 adds
    `s3_gateway_multipart_part.encryption_iv` and `key_version`.
    CompleteMultipartUpload authenticates every part, then streams them into
    the vault object (no `.complete-object` concatenation file); a failed part
    answers `InvalidPart` and keeps the upload.
  * Startup seals pre-#345 plaintext parts after an MD5 and size check, deletes
    the plaintext, and removes files and directories no open upload references.
  * `ObjectStore::putObjectFromFile` and `MultipartStore::uploadPartFromFile`
    are replaced by `putObjectFromSource` and `uploadPartFromSource`
    (`fs::PlaintextSource`).
  * KeyProvider holds its secret in crypto::SecretBytes (mlocked, zeroed on
    release); getMasterKey() returns std::span; init()/updateMasterKey() take
    spans (#350).
  * AES-GCM helpers take key (and encrypt plaintext) as spans; vault-key,
    retained-key, pepper and attestation-seed paths keep no plain vector
    copies; transient copies use crypto::WipedKeyCopy.
  * TPM sealer zeroes its command and response buffers and refuses secrets
    larger than the TPM's sealed-data limit; the DB password is built per
    connect and wiped.
  * platform::sealer: Policy {None, Pcr7}; TPM seal with PolicyPCR over PCR 7
    (no USERWITHAUTH), unseal through a policy session, typed PolicyUnsatisfied
    on TPM_RC_POLICY_FAIL; staged, verified reseal() (<name>.staged.*,
    <name>.prev.*) with recover() run by KeyProvider::init/initGuarded; macOS
    ignores the policy (#351).
  * secrets.tpm_pcr_policy none|pcr7 (default none) binds newly sealed secrets
    only; crypto/secrets/Reseal (inventory, run) never calls init/initGuarded.
  * `vh secret reseal [--pcr7|--none]` (gate: hardware posture, vault key
    backups, `vh secret export all` < 24 h; audited secrets.reseal,
    confirmation sealing_policy_reseal, stepup Secrets, excluded from MCP) and
    `vh secret policy`; migration 132 secret_exports records exports; health
    deps.secrets_policy, secrets_pcr_bound, secrets_warning (degraded when
    PCR-bound and no export in 30 days).
  * Key attestation key and the DB password are never regenerated on
    PolicyUnsatisfied.
  * Versioned vault_master rewrap (#352): vh secret master
    status|rotate|finalize|rollback; staged, verified, atomic switch with a
    bounded recovery window (default 7 days); file bodies are never
    re-encrypted.
  * Migration 121: vault_master_state, master_version / staged (*_next) /
    retained (*_prev) wrap columns on vault_keys, vault_keys_trashed and
    vault_deletion_key, and trigger vh_vault_master_guard (a write must declare
    the current master).
  * Versioned rewrap for ak_master and s3gw_master (#353): the #352 engine
    generalized to every data-wrapping master (crypto/secrets/Rewrap.hpp,
    crypto/secrets/Versioned.hpp); vh secret master
    status|rotate|finalize|rollback --slot vault|api-keys|s3-gateway (status
    also --slot all); every recovery window ends from the vault retention loop.
  * vh secret jwt rotate: a fresh JWT secret and the revocation of every
    refresh token in one transaction, then every live session ends. vh secret
    device-identity rotate: a new device_tls version sealed and read back, then
    one transaction revokes every paired device and switches; pairing state is
    dropped and the listener swaps its TLS context live.
  * Migration 124: master_secret_state (one row per slot; vault_master_state
    moved into it and dropped), master_version / staged (*_next) / retained
    (*_prev) wrap columns on api_keys (secret and provider token) and
    s3_gateway_credentials, guard triggers vh_api_keys_master_guard and
    vh_s3_gateway_master_guard; the rewrap exemption is now vh.master_rewrap =
    '<slot>'.
  * Refresh token rows record session assurance: aal, amr, auth_time (migration
    119; existing rows read as aal 1, {pwd}, auth_time = issued_at). Rows are
    written once per jti; promotion never adopts an existing row (#354).
  * A websocket rehydrates only from its cookie's stored row, never from
    another live socket's token; auth.login on a signed-in socket answers
    data.code already_signed_in.
  * MFA policy hook (auth::session::assurance) checked against the row on
    rehydration, validate, auth.refresh and the HTTP lanes; refused rows are
    revoked. Policy and factor changes bump rbac::policyEpoch().
  * New ws commands auth.sessions.list (opaque ids) and auth.sessions.revoke
    {id} | {all_others}; audit event auth.session.revoke; console Sessions
    panel under Account › Security.
  * Console sign-in reconnects its websocket first, so the signing-in socket
    owns the browser's current refresh cookie; already_signed_in falls back to
    auth.refresh.
  * WebAuthn relying-party verifier (#355, design docs/dev/mfa-design.md
    section 4.1, decision D5) in `core/{include,src}/auth/webauthn/`, built as
    its own static library (`vh_webauthn`) over libcbor, OpenSSL 3 and
    libsodium only: registration (`none`, `packed` self and `packed` x5c, other
    formats recorded as none) and assertions for ES256, EdDSA (Ed25519) and
    RS256 (2048..4096 bits); all 14 checks of the design table, including the
    session/purpose/user/scope-bound single-use challenge store, exact
    configured origins, crossOrigin/topOrigin, UP and UV, credential owner and
    userHandle, BE immutability and the backup-aware signCount table. Strict
    CBOR (definite lengths, no trailing bytes, no duplicate keys, bounded
    nesting and sizes), strict clientDataJSON and response JSON. Not wired into
    sign-in yet (#357).
  * New config `auth.webauthn.{rp_id,rp_name,origins}` (config.yaml,
    settings.get/update, ws contract model `WebAuthnConfig`, console Settings ›
    Security), validated on load and on settings.update.
  * Packaging: Build-Depends `libcbor-dev (>= 0.10)`; the runtime `libcbor0.10`
    comes through `${shlibs:Depends}`. macOS: Homebrew `libcbor` (bundled into
    the pkg by `bundle_dylibs.py`). The CI image adds `libcbor-dev`, `clang-18`
