    `SqlDeployerHistoryDb.StorageLayoutMigration*`,
    `SqlDeployerHistoryDb.LayoutCheckConstraint*`.
  * VHF1 at rest for new writes (#367, WS2-b; design/encryption/02 §3.7, §3.8,
    §4): every new content generation is VHF1 (`EncryptionManager`
    encrypt/encryptToFile/reseal/rotation, FUSE seals, `createFile`,
    hydrate-and-seal of plaintext objects, provider restores, key salvage, the
    resumable sealing cursor). The generation id (`files.encryption_iv`,
    `recovery_blob.encryption_iv`, upload rows, `x-amz-meta-vh-iv`, the remote
    index) is the header salt; its length tells the formats apart
    (`vault::content::formatOf`: 12-byte IV = legacy GCM, 32-byte salt = VHF1).
    Legacy GCM stays readable everywhere and is not rewritten.
  * Reads: `storage::Vhf1FileReader` (per-chunk authentication, catalog size as
    the authenticated object size, sticky failures) behind
    `Engine::openPlaintextReader`; `RemoteRangedReader` authenticates VHF1
    windows (header GET at open); hydrate verifies VHF1 with a full chunk walk.
    No VHF1 path calls `gcmCtrDecryptAt`
    (`crypto::util::gcmCtrDecryptAtCallsForTesting`,
    `Vhf1AtRestTest.IntegrityContract*`).
  * Resumable uploads: `vault::content::PrefixSealer` seals whole chunks as the
    prefix grows, resumes by authenticating the recorded prefix, and continues
    under a fresh salt (`<ord>.sealed.resalt`, `Resumable::setSealing`) when
    bytes past the record or divergent stored bytes would otherwise reseal an
    index under one salt. Uploads created before this release keep the legacy
    cursor.
  * Size math is format-aware everywhere it was `plaintext + 16`
    (repairAtRest's size correction, hydrate/ranged expectations, provider
    version sizes and own-write detection, rotation's re-seal check, conflict
    preview caps, restore and snapshot estimates). `crypto::util::b64_decode`
    decodes any length (it was capped at 12 bytes).
  * Migration 126: generated `files.content_format` and
