    `SqlDeployerHistoryDb.ManifestV2MigrationIsAdditiveAndIdempotent`.
  * `opaque_v1` data plane (#369, WS1-b; design/encryption/01 §4, §5, §7):
    content objects are `o/<file uid>` (one object per file; provider versions
    are content versions) beside the `.vaulthalla/` manifest objects; key
    construction and parsing live in `storage/ObjectKeys.hpp`. Every request of
    an `opaque_v1` vault is held to spec 01 §4.3 in one place,
    `s3::Controller::enforceRequestPolicy` (set by `CloudEngine` for the
    vault's layout), before it is sent or metered: keys only `o/<uid>` and
    `.vaulthalla/{layout.json,root,seg/<sid>,key/<kid>}`, listings only with
    the prefixes "", `o/`, `.vaulthalla/…` or one allowlisted key, no
    `x-amz-meta-*`, `Content-Type: application/octet-stream` only (multipart
    initiates send it too), no content headers or tags, no metadata rewrites,
    CopyObject onto its own key only (provider-version restore). Checksums stay
    the SigV4 payload hash of the bytes on the wire (ciphertext). Refusals
    throw `s3::OpaqueRequestRefused`.
  * Sync is keyed by file uid under `opaque_v1`: both sides of a pass are keyed
    `/o/<uid>`, a remote row is bound to the file with that uid (so a rename or
    move is no difference and sends no object request; the encrypted manifest
    takes it at the next publish), the planner treats a row naming the file's
    generation as in sync, and remote objects no file names are left alone
    (trashed files' objects wait for the purge; orphans are counted and logged,
    never downloaded or deleted). A full listing has no prefix and sorts
    content, metadata and foreign keys; foreign objects are ignored and
    reported (`foreign_objects = ignore`, the default) or imported once as
    `/Imported/<key>` (`import`, `CloudEngine::importForeignObject`, the only
    request that names a foreign key, inside `s3::ScopedForeignObjectRead`).
    Events: foreign keys are never HEADed or indexed; an `o/<uid>` object no
    file of the vault has is an orphan.
  * The remote index, provider versions (`upstream_object_version`,
    `file_versions.upstream_key`), write intents, the usage ledger and the
    backlog replay use `o/<uid>` keys. Uploads record the PUT's ETag and
    version id on the index row (and the upload baseline), which the manifest
    collector turns into the entry's object reference (sealed size, ETag,
    version id) and generation (`format: 1`, hex salt). Trash purges remove the
    index row by object key.
  * Reads of `opaque_v1` objects are bound to the vault and the file's uid,
    mandatory (sync downloads, hydrate, ranged reads, conflict previews,
    provider restores, key salvage, local VHF1 reads): a row without a recorded
    generation learns it from the authenticated header. Hydrate is one GET (no
    metadata HEAD); `CloudEngine::requireOpaqueHeader` refuses to upload bytes
    whose header doesn't name the file. Copies re-seal under the copy's own uid
    and a new generation. Provider restores of `opaque_v1` versions take the
    generation from Vaulthalla's own write (or the header) and re-seal a
    transfer into the destination's uid; directory points and point-in-time
    plans place objects by the paths the database gives their uids.
  * Manifest v2: a pass that changed nothing costs one HEAD of the root
    (`ObjectStore::headEtag`, `Service::verifyRemote`) and publishes nothing
    (`Service::publishIfChanged`); explicit publishes are unchanged.
  * Multipart part size `max(5 MiB, ceil(size / 10,000))` rounded up to a whole
    MiB for every layout (`object_keys::partSizeFor`): objects up to 48.8 GiB
    keep 5 MiB parts, larger ones stay within 10,000 parts.
  * `opaque_v1` creation stays refused outside test mode (#371 lifts it);
    `path_v0` and `transparent` vaults behave as before.
  * Tests: `OpaqueObjectKeys.*`, `OpaqueRequestPolicy.*`,
    `OpaqueMultipart.ASixtyGiBObjectUploadsInAtMost10000Parts` (a sparse 60 GiB
    object), `OpaqueLayoutTest.*` (`OpaqueRequestAudit`, `OpaqueObjectCount`,
    `OpaqueRenameCostsNothing`, `OpaqueRequestCountsMatchSpec01Section5`,
    `OpaqueCopyReseals`, `OpaqueReadsRequireTheFilesObjectUid`,
    `OpaqueForeignObjectsAndOrphans`,
    `OpaqueManifestNamesTheObjectsItsFilesAreStoredIn`,
    `OpaqueCreationIsRefusedOutsideTestMode`) and
    `LiveR2.OpaqueObjectsCarryNoMetadataAndCostWhatSpec01Says`. The versioned
    fake bucket records every request (`VersionedBucket::requests()`) and runs
    the request policy like the network paths; the audit enrolls a recovery
    recipient, so its KEV1 envelopes (`.vaulthalla/key/<kid>`, #370) go through
    the same rules, whose controller `vault::envelopes` now sets to the vault's
    layout too.
  * Recovery recipients and KEV1 key envelopes (#370, WS3-b;
    design/encryption/03 §5): customer-held X25519 recipients per S3 vault;
    every held and future key version is wrapped (KEV1 suite 1, purpose 1,
    recipient class 0) to every usable grant and PUT to the vault's bucket as
    `.vaulthalla/key/<kid>` (`If-None-Match: *`). Rotations, the salvage key
    and a vault's first key write their envelopes before the version is
    committed (a failed PUT fails the rotation); enrollment wraps every held
    version; each sync pass writes what is missing. The manifest v2 root lists
    the grants (`recovery_policy`, revoked ones with `revoked_at`) and
    `keys[].envelopes`. The KEV1 reader rejects reserved purposes and recipient
    classes, which a restore skips.
  * `vh vault keys recipient list|add|revoke` and ws
    `vault.keys.recipients.list|add|revoke` (add and revoke: CLI or a paired
    device only, `recipient_requires_device` elsewhere, agents denied and
    excluded from MCP; keys step-up bound to the vault, #359; encryption-key
    Export permission). Audit events `vault.recovery.recipient.add|revoke`.
  * `vh vault keys import --recovery-kit <file|->` (read client-side): unwraps
    the vault's envelopes with the kit's seed and restores only versions the
    vault's records reference whose recorded (or manifest-root) attestation by
    this node verifies; planted keys that don't match the recorded key check
    are ignored; recover mode only. Audit event
    `vault.keys.import.recovery_kit`.
  * New offline tool `vh-recovery` (`/usr/bin/vh-recovery`):
    `kit create|public-key|add-vault|show`. Kit format `vh.recovery-kit` v1.
  * D2 (spec 03 §5.5): `vault.keys.status` gains `recovery`
    (`not_required|recipient|export|recovery_material_missing`, uncovered
    versions, `rotation_needed`), `vault_uid`, `node_id`,
    `attestation_public_keys` and `checkpoint`; health gains
    `vaults_recovery_material_missing` (degraded). Exports' attestations carry
    `public_key`; an import checks it against this node's key of that id.
  * Migration 127: `vault_recovery_grant` (class constrained to `customer`,
    suite 1) and `vault_key_envelope`. Additive and idempotent.
  * Tests: `Kev1Vectors`, `Kev1ReservedFields`, `RecoverySeedVectors`,
    `RecoveryKit`, `RecoveryRecipient.*`, `RecoveryRequirement.*`,
    `RecoveryNoSilentCustody.*`, `KeysImportFromKit.*`,
    `VaultKeyExportProvenance.*`, `recovery_tool`.
  * `vh vault recover` and ws `vault.recover` (#371, WS3-c;
    design/encryption/03 §6.4): rebuild an `opaque_v1` vault on any instance
    from its bucket plus a recovery kit and/or a key export. D1 trust anchor: a
    key version is canonical only when its original node's attestation verifies
    against an attestation key pinned in the customer's material (never the
    restoring node's own); otherwise `salvage_required`, and `--salvage` (typed
    confirmation `vault_recover_salvage`) re-encrypts under a fresh key above
    every version the bucket names without storing the supplied keys.
    `--dry-run` writes nothing. Restored vaults start in the cache strategy:
    only manifest objects, envelopes and 120-byte headers are read until a file
    is. Paired devices need the keys step-up; browser sessions get
    `recover_requires_device`; excluded from MCP. Audit event `vault.recover`.
  * Migration 131 `vault_key_origin`: where a recovered key version was minted
    and the original attestation; reported by `vault.keys.status`
    (`needed_versions[].origin`) and carried in the manifest root
    (`keys[].origin`). Root grants carry their public key
    (`recovery_policy.grants[].public_key`), so a restore re-enrolls them.
  * New encrypting S3 vaults are created `opaque_v1` (the internal staging is
    lifted: the restore drill passes). Existing `path_v0` vaults are unchanged
    until the layout migration (WS1-c).
  * Key exports carry `node_id` and `vault_uid`. Key rotation and salvage
    record the object they PUT in the remote index, so the `opaque_v1` manifest
    names the object the bucket holds after a rotation.
  * Harness: `vhh drill recover` (spec 03 §7: three private instances with
    their own databases and swtpm, a fake S3 with versioning and a request log,
    negative drills) and `tools/harness/drill_recover_r2.sh` (live R2, fixed
    test bucket `vh-test-recover-drill`, deleted afterwards).
  * Local format conversion to VHF1 (#372, WS2-c; design/encryption/02 §6.1,
    §6.2): `vault::conversion` (`vault/FormatConversion.hpp`) re-seals legacy
    (gcm0) live backing files and `.vh-recovery` blobs as VHF1 under the same
    key version, verified per item (legacy tag over the whole input, plaintext
    BLAKE2b while streaming, full VHF1 chunk walk bound to vault/salt/key
    version/object, matching digest and recorded size) before anything is
    replaced. Live files use the rotation sidecar protocol under the content
    lock (sidecar → one transaction: files CAS + the shared blob's row → rename
    → unlink the old blob); a blob of a live file's generation is linked to the
    converted sidecar and converts once. Other blobs: new store name → CAS of
    the `recovery_blob` row → unlink. Crash recovery is the existing startup
    sidecar recovery and store sweep. `content_hash` columns are kept, as
    rotation keeps them.
  * `vault::FormatConversionService` (runtime service, after
    VaultRetentionService): passes every
    `vaults.format_conversion.pass_interval` over vaults with legacy rows,
    paced by `max_mib_per_second` and `max_cpu_percent` (pauses between items),
    blocked while the vault rotates its key or a salvage runs. Live files of
    encrypt-upstream cloud vaults are left to the layout migration (spec 01
    §6); rows of plaintext-upstream vaults without local bytes get their stale
    legacy id cleared, as rotation does.
  * `vh vault format status <vault|all> [--json]` (`ops::vault_format`,
    encryption-key View permission): remaining live files and blobs (items,
    bytes), state, last pass. `db::query::vault::FormatConversion` uses
    migration 126's `content_format` columns.
  * Tests: `FormatMigrationTest.*` (DB-free: digests, crash at every step +
    restart, hard links once, no plaintext on disk, refusals, rotation/salvage,
    pacing), `FormatMigrationDbTest.*` (db-only: snapshots, local versions and
    retired-key generations restore after conversion; status; blocked during
    rotation).
  * Bucket layout migration `path_v0` → `opaque_v1` (#373, WS1-c;
    design/encryption/01 §6 as simplified on the issue, 2026-11-06: no
    soak/rollback, no `encrypt_upstream` toggling): `vault::layout`
    (`vault/LayoutMigration.hpp`), `vault::LayoutMigrationService` (runtime
    service after FormatConversionService),
    `db::query::vault::LayoutMigration`, migration 133 `vault_layout_migration`
    + `vault_layout_migration_item`. Operator-started only:
    `vh vault migrate-layout <vault> [--status|--cleanup [--confirm <name>]] [--accept-unpriced]`,
    ws `vault.layout.status` / `.migrate` (keys step-up) / `.cleanup`
    (data.destroy step-up with confirm); encryption-key View / Rotate
    permission. Audit events `vault.layout.migrate`, `vault.layout.cutover`,
    `vault.layout.failed`, `vault.layout.cleanup`, `vault.layout.cleanup.run`.
  * The run holds the vault's sync pass lease from the inventory to the
    cutover: complete ListObjectVersions (R2: ListObjectsV2) inventory, one
    HEAD per legacy version, `LayoutMigrationCost` estimate + price preflight
    (`layout_migration`), then per object oldest first: legacy versions (GET
    `?versionId` If-Match), pending backlog generations and the current content
    re-sealed as VHF1 bound to the file uid under the same key version (gcm0
    local copies through the #372 live-file protocol first), PUT `o/<uid>`
    without metadata, delete markers carried as DELETE of `o/<uid>`; proof of
    storage by MD5/multipart-MD5 ETag or read-back; random sample read-back
    (max(1 %, 100)); a second complete listing; one cutover transaction
    (layout, index-only rows' generations by compare-and-set, no live gcm0 row
    or pending gcm0 backlog blob allowed, remote index and baselines on
    `o/<uid>`, backlog and sent local versions remapped, re-sealed versions
    keep their original `last_modified`); then manifest v2 publish and a
    bucket-only load check. A bucket changed since the last sync refuses
    (`diverged`). Failures before the cutover leave `path_v0` (state `failed`,
    resumable).
  * Cleanup after `cleanup_after` (default 7 days) with the manifest verified,
    never on a protected bucket: dry run, then explicit version-id deletes
    (`s3::Controller::deleteObjectVersion`; unversioned: DELETE after a HEAD
    matched the inventoried ETag) of exactly the inventoried,
    verified/superseded legacy versions; Object Lock retention and legal holds
    read by `HEAD ?versionId`
    (`ObjectVersionHead::{object_lock_mode,retain_until,legal_hold}`) and
    respected; refused deletes (`s3::DeleteRefused`) stay `cleanup_blocked`
    with their reason, retried daily; `complete` only after a complete listing
    shows no legacy key, version or marker.
  * `s3::ScopedLegacyLayoutCleanup` lets the cleanup HEAD/DELETE inventoried
    legacy keys of an opaque_v1 controller (never PUT/COPY).
    `ObjectKeyPage::objects` (ETag, size, last modified). path_v0 listings and
    the event applier ignore `o/<uid>` objects (the migration's, before the
    cutover). `ObjectIdentity::pathOfUid` falls back to the migrated legacy key
    for history whose file is gone. `encrypt_upstream` can't change while a
    migration copies.
  * Health `legacy_content` {remaining, path_v0_vaults,
    layout_migrations_unfinished, legacy_files, legacy_blobs,
    legacy_upload_files} (#376 readiness; informational, never degrades).
    Removed with no callers left: `crypto/util/reseal.{hpp,cpp}` (the gcm0→gcm0
    re-sealer), `crypto/util/verify.{hpp,cpp}`
    (`aes256_gcm_file_authenticates`), `crypto::util::read_file`. Legacy
    content is only read now.
  * Config `vaults.layout_migration.cleanup_wait_days` (1..365, default 7).
    Test-instance env `VH_TEST_NEW_ENCRYPTING_LAYOUT=path_v0` (test mode only).
    Harness `vhh drill migrate`; unit suites `LayoutMigration*`, live
    `LiveR2.LayoutMigrationPrimitives`.
  * The host master slot ('master') opens with KeyProvider::initGuarded (#393):
    new dependents::internalSecrets() counts the internal_secrets rows it wraps
    (all but jwt_secret and the plaintext pre-#322 pepper); a missing sealed
    blob with such rows throws MasterSecretLost (the daemon doesn't start) with
    the restore and give-up steps, and nothing is sealed in its place.
  * Test tooling: the Tier B harness and the lab ws clients (`vhws.py`,
    `parity_smoke.py`, `ws_churn.py`, the pentest fixture) sign admins in with
    a TOTP authenticator and answer step-up refusals with the password and a
    code, the same flow the console runs. Nothing in the daemon changed, and no
    test bypass was added (#395).
  * The daemon drops its cached ws sessions before main returns
    (`runtime::Deps::releaseSessions()`), and an io_context kept for sessions
    that outlived their listener is never freed: a session destroyed during
    static destruction could crash the process at exit (SIGSEGV in the
    websocket stream on Linux, "mutex lock failed" abort on macOS) (#397,
    #385).
  * A share session's identity (principal, session id, token) is read and
    replaced under a lock; concurrent HTTP and resumable-upload threads could
    read a torn token ("Malformed share token prefix") (#389).
  * Device `/transfer/source` releases its calibration slot as the last byte is
    produced, so a client never sees its finished request still counted against
    the two-at-once limit (#389).
  * The upstream event applier no longer records a HEAD cancelled by shutdown
    as a failure: the row stays pending with no attempt burned, no backoff and
    no database write while stopping (#412).
  * Unit tests: a suite that leaves sessions in the process-wide session
    manager fails at its own end; device suites shut down their resumable
    upload workers and io_contexts; the fake event queue long-polls like SQS
    (#385, #389, #397, #412).
  * Build: the preview helpers (`vaulthalla-preview-cad`,
    `vaulthalla-preview-media`, `vh_helper_common`) are never sanitized, even
    in a `-Db_sanitize` build: a sanitized helper can't start under its own
    `RLIMIT_AS` cap. `bin/tests/run_unit.sh` builds the helpers the suites
    spawn (`core/vh_unit_test_helpers`), and `itest_ensure_build` re-configures
    when a meson.build changes.
  * `vh secret set mfa-seal|mfa-pepper <file>` (#398) imports the export of the
    MFA keys on a new host, so a restored database keeps TOTP and recovery
    codes working with no re-enrollment. Super admins only, confirmed, audited
    as `secrets.mfa_key.import` (`ops::mfa_keys`); the key is checked before it
    is adopted (a stored TOTP seed must open, or the hash of one of the actor's
    own recovery codes, `--code`, must recompute), a mismatched key or a host
    that already has a working key is refused with nothing changed, and the key
    is never printed.
  * Test tooling: the Tier B harness's console lane replaces an existing file
    with `PUT /upload/content` and `If-Match`, as a client should, instead of
    sending it to the create-only upload session; a 412 is a concurrent-write
    conflict. Nothing in the daemon changed (#404).
  * Test tooling: the macOS install acceptance and storage smoke and the web
    e2e suite sign admins in with the real second factor (TOTP, first
    enrollment) and answer step-up refusals; the e2e account's authenticator is
    enrolled at provisioning (#405). No daemon change.
  * Test tooling: the web e2e suite matches the console again (#414). The
    recovery spec expects point-in-time restore for S3 folders only, as the
    console and daemon do, and covers both sides. The PDF paging spec follows
    the entry-id preview URLs from #327. The passkey sign-in spec runs as its
    own Playwright project after the others, so parallel workers no longer use
    up its per-address passkey rate limit. `provision_e2e_user.sh` no longer
    reports a local-DB seed it skipped when
    `VAULTHALLA_E2E_ALLOW_LOCAL_DEV_DB_SEED=1` names no database. No product
    change.
  * `/preview/batch` returns `entry=<id>` preview URLs (vault and `share=1`)
    instead of `path=` URLs (#417); they resolve through the #327 Access rules
    and the share scope check.
    `HttpAccessDbTest.PreviewBatchAnswersFromTheIndexAndDecodesOncePerSource`
    pins that no batch URL carries `path=` or a file name. The console already
    uses the returned URLs as is, so no web change.

