vaulthalla (1.16.0-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Devices
    - Add the device listener (runtime service DeviceServer, port 36972): TLS
      1.3 only, client certificate required, authorization by the client key's
      SPKI SHA-256 at handshake time; /ws reaches the ws router as a Device
      session (the device's user, no tokens) or a pairing-only session
      (device.pair.* and meta.contract.get; anything else answers data.code
      device_unknown).
    - Seal the listener's ECDSA P-256 key through the secrets KeyProvider
      (device_tls) on first start; keep its self-signed certificate as
      device_server_certificate.pem in the state directory.
    - Add migration 112: devices (user_id ON DELETE CASCADE, approved_by ON
      DELETE SET NULL, unique current and previous key) and device_node.
      Pairing windows and requests stay in memory.
    - Add ops::devices with ws device.pair.request|confirm|status,
      device.identity.rotate, device.server.identity,
      device.pairing.open|close|pending|approve|deny, device.list|rename|revoke
      and device.remote.status|enable|disable, and the CLI vh device
      pair|pending|approve|deny|list|rename|revoke|remote
      status|enable|disable.
    - Number matching: pending requests offer three codes (one is the
      device's), and any wrong code denies the request.
    - Add config section device_server (enabled, port, remote,
      max_connections); remote defaults to off (loopback) and is written only
      by vh device remote enable|disable, which rebinds the listener without a
      restart.
    - auth revokeSessions closes live device sessions; device revocation closes
      them immediately.
    - Audit log lines written while a device's command runs end with "[via
      device <id>]".
    - Serve /preview*, /download* and /upload* on the device listener over TLS
      as the device's user (no cookies, no share lanes) and remote MCP at /mcp
      (Streamable HTTP: POST, JSON responses, Origin and MCP-Protocol-Version
      checks, the vh mcp catalog run through the CLI router, 10-minute call
      deadline). The HTTP session runs over a plain or TLS byte stream; device
      HTTP connections end on revocation, revokeSessions or remote-off.
    - MCP never offers vh device pair|approve|deny or vh device remote
      enable|disable; every vh device leaf declares its effect.
    - device.pairing.close closes only the caller's window unless a super admin
      passes any (CLI: vh device pair --close --any); device.pairing.open and
      .pending return server_time, and .open the listener's port and remote.
  * Web console
    - Add Devices (/devices, any signed-in user): own devices with rename,
      revoke (confirm) and copyable ids, and the lazy "Pair a device" dialog
      (device.pairing.open, device.pairing.pending polled every 2 s while
      visible, number matching via device.pairing.approve, deny, a countdown by
      the node's clock (server_time), the listener port from
      device.pairing.open, device.pairing.close on dismiss; another user's
      window is shown, never closed).
    - Add /devices/all for super admins: device.list {all} with owners and the
      remote-connections card (device.remote.status|enable|disable); Settings
      shows device_server as managed there.
    - Add tests/e2e/devices.spec.ts, which also runs with no daemon
      (VAULTHALLA_E2E_OFFLINE=1).
  * Vault keys
    - Add ops::vault_keys with ws vault.keys.status, vault.keys.export and
      vault.keys.export.confirm and the CLI
      `vh vault keys status <vault|all> [--json]`; `vh vault keys export` goes
      through the same op.
    - Key check: BLAKE2b-128 (crypto_generichash) keyed by the vault key over
      "vh-key-check-v1" ‖ vault_id (ASCII decimal) ‖ UInt32BE(version),
      lowercase hex; status and exports carry it per version, never key bytes
      in status.
    - Exports carry every needed version under historical_keys (key_check,
      reason): an unfinished rotation's outgoing key (it used to be omitted
      mid-rotation), recovery-referenced retired keys, and for deleted vaults
      too.
    - vault.keys.export and .export.confirm answer only paired device sessions
      (data.code export_requires_device for any other session); confirm records
      the export only while the version is current and the key check matches.
    - Add
      `vh vault keys import <vault> --file <path|-> [--salvage] [--confirm-name] [--json]`
      and ws vault.keys.import {vault_id, keys, mode?, confirm_name?} (paired
      devices only, import_requires_device elsewhere). Recover (default)
      restores, per version, only versions the daemon's records reference (key
      rows, retired rows and their successor, deleted-vault copies, data sealed
      under it) whose attestation by this node verifies (or that match a key
      the host still unseals); outcomes restored | unchanged | needs_salvage |
      refused. Never replaces a key the host unseals (conflict), never
      introduces a version or moves the current one (with no key row, current =
      the highest referenced version). NeedsConfirmation vault_key_import; one
      transaction under the rotation lock; then reloads or starts the vault's
      engine.
    - Key provenance (migration 113): vault_keys, vault_keys_trashed and
      vault_deletion_key gain key_check, attestation_key_id and attestation,
      recorded on creation and rotation and backfilled whenever the daemon
      unseals a version; key_attestation_keys holds this node's Ed25519 public
      keys (the seed is sealed as key_attest and never exported; a seed that no
      longer unseals is replaced by a new keypair, old public keys stay).
      Attestation = Ed25519 over "vh-key-attest-v1" ‖ node_id ‖
      UInt64BE(vault_id) ‖ UInt32BE(version) ‖ key_check. Status and exports
      carry {key_id, signature} per version.
    - Salvage (mode salvage, `--salvage`, NeedsConfirmation vault_key_salvage):
      for versions that need it, mints a fresh attested key version through the
      rotation path, re-encrypts every file the supplied key authenticates with
      the rotation's sidecar protocol (remote-only objects through the metered
      fetch, price preflight key_salvage, refused → skipped_remote with the
      estimate), reports the rest (unrecoverable, deferred, failed; first 50
      failures) in vault_key_salvage, the import result and status
      last_salvage, and never stores the supplied key. An interrupted pass
      resumes on the same key. Versions a salvage names no longer keep the
      vault from starting or a rotation from finishing.
    - EncryptionManager::load_key no longer mints a key for a vault with sealed
      content (files with an IV, recovery blobs, live provider versions,
      encrypted remote index entries): a missing vault_keys row, a row that
      doesn't unseal, or a missing version live files or a pending rotation
      need throws vault::KeyUnavailable (key_missing | key_unsealable |
      version_missing); the engine stays down, storage::Manager records the
      vault, stats.system.health gains vaults_unavailable (degraded) and vh
      status lists it; vault.keys.status reports the code as unavailable_reason
      plus unavailable_detail.
    - The vh client reads the import file itself and attaches it to the request
      (`input` member, at most 512 KiB); the daemon never opens a caller-named
      path for it.
    - MCP: key commands refuse when run for an MCP client (remote /mcp, or a vh
      mcp request marked agent), in addition to the catalog exclusions.
    - ws contract: vault.keys.* responses are models (VaultKeyStatus,
      VaultKeyNeededVersion, VaultKeyExport, VaultKeyImportResult,
      VaultKeyAttestation, VaultKeySalvageReport, VaultKeySalvageFailure,
      checked against their serializers); import's keys are
      VaultKeyImportDocument (exactly what the import reads, checked against
      its parser; other members ignored, so a whole export is one); import
      declares the vault_key_import and vault_key_salvage confirmations
      (accept_field confirm_name) and SystemHealth declares vaults_unavailable.
  * Vaults
    - Add ops::vaults::listAccessible with ws storage.vault.accessible.list
      (human and device sessions) and `vh vault list --accessible [--json]`
      (operation vaults.accessible.list, MCP tool vaults_accessible_list):
      every live vault the actor reaches as owner, by a vault role of its own
      or a group's, by its admin role, or as super admin. Rows carry a vault
      summary (model AccessibleVault) plus browse (rbac::resolver::Vault,
      FilesystemAction::List at the root's FUSE path, as fs.dir.list enforces
      it), manage (admin vault View, as storage.vault.get) and via (owner |
      vault_role | group_role | admin_policy | super_admin). A deactivated
      account gets an empty list.
    - Filesystem denials found while listing are not logged
      (rbac::resolver::vault::QuietFilesystemDenials); refused requests still
      are.
    - Add db::query::vault::Vault::listLiveVaults (unpaginated). Storage engine
      startup, vault key status/export of all vaults and S3 gateway vault name
      resolution used listVaults' default 100-row page and silently skipped
      later vaults.
    - storage::Manager::addVault drops an engine already registered under the
      vault's id from the path index, and initStorageEngines clears the id
      index too: one engine per vault id. A test process that reset its schema
      reused ids, and ops::vault_keys (which scans getEngines()) could take
      another vault's engine and key
      (DeviceHttpTest.VaultKeyExportOnlyOnAPairedDevice on macOS); covered by
      StorageEngineRegistryTest.
  * CLI
    - Add `vh mcp`: an MCP server (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, protocol
      2025-11-25, also 2025-06-18 and 2025-03-26).
      + Tools: `vh_help` (usage book), `vh_read` (read-only commands), `vh_run`
        (any agent-available command) and one typed tool per catalogued
        operation with a CLI binding, annotated readOnly/destructive from the
        command's effect.
      + Each tool call is a non-interactive control-socket request authorized
        by peer UID like any `vh` run; `--json` is added where supported;
        confirmation flags are only passed from the agent's explicit `confirm`
        argument.
      + A command with effect `destructive` is refused before it runs unless
        `confirm` carries `"destructive": true`, independent of the command's
        own prompt (closes the non-interactive `vault delete` path for agents).
      + A refusal for want of a confirmation becomes a `needs_confirmation`
        result, from the result frame's new `confirmation` member (text
        matching only for older daemons).
      + Calls run concurrently (ping and cancellation are never queued behind
        one); `notifications/cancelled` closes the call's connection; a call is
        abandoned after `VAULTHALLA_MCP_TIMEOUT` seconds (default 600).
      + Never exposed: `vault keys export`, `secrets export|update`, `setup …`,
        `teardown …`, `fs get|put`.
    - Add
      `vh skill install --agent claude|codex [--scope user|project] [--no-register] [--force]`:
      writes the `vaulthalla` skill (`SKILL.md`, `references/commands.md`) to
      `~/.claude/skills` / `~/.agents/skills` (or the project's
      `.claude/skills` / `.agents/skills`) and registers `vh mcp` via
      `claude mcp add` / `codex mcp add`; idempotent, replaces a changed skill
      only with `--force`, needs no daemon.
    - The control socket's `result` frame gains an optional `confirmation`
      member (`code`, `flag`, `flags`) when a command refused because nobody
      confirmed it; the CLI ignores it.
    - Every CLI leaf declares its effect (`read`, `write`, `destructive`),
      exported as `effect` by `vh_usage --json`.
  * WS contract
    - ws contract: the usage book (`core/usage/src/contract/ws`) declares every
      registered ws command (223, was 76).
      `UsageContract.EveryRegisteredWsCommandHasABookEntry` and
      `test_ws_contract.py` fail on a command without one.
    - web: client timeouts come only from the contract; WebSocketCommandMap
      payload types follow the server (no `null` payloads, no members the
      handlers never read).
    - ws contract version 2: `models[]` and `models_hash` (170 named model
      shapes, `core/usage/src/contract/models`), field `one_of` / `values` /
      `element` / `partial`, command `response_model`; `meta.contract.get`
      answers `models_hash`. `ContractModels.*` checks every model against its
      serializer with seeded instances;
      `UsageContract.UnionsObjectsAndArraysAreSpelledOut` keeps unions and
      objects typed (free-form JSON is an explicit allowlist).
    - Handler JSON builders the contract tests need are public and
      database-free (S3 gateway, vault details and removal plan, share entries
      and links, email history, vault stats); their output is unchanged.
    - ws contract: commands list `refusal_kinds` and `confirmations`
      (`{code, accept_field, description}`, 20 codes); `refusal_codes` stays
      their union. Kinds are the generic ones plus the device pairing codes
      (`no_pairing_window`, `rate_limited`, `device_unknown`) and, on the vault
      key commands only, `export_requires_device` / `import_requires_device`.
      Generated TS exports `WS_CONTRACT_CONFIRMATIONS`.
    - ws: fs, share, pricing, email, settings, S3 gateway, vault/API key,
      permission and dashboard-preference refusals carry `data.code` (untyped
      refusals: 61 commands, now 2); messages unchanged. Share Manager,
      TargetResolver and PrincipalResolver throw ops refusal types; HTTP share
      lanes are unaffected.
    - ws: unroutable frames are answered `parse_error` with `error` (and
      `message`, kept for old clients) and the frame's requestId; router faults
      answer `<command>.response` with status ERROR instead of `UNKNOWN` /
      `Unknown.response`; a non-string requestId no longer breaks an answer; a
      missing `payload` reads as `{}`.
    - ws contract: `envelope` documents the request, answer and error frames.
  * Security fixes
    - RBAC: the filesystem evaluator's last stage (global vault policy) tried
      the self, admin and user scopes in turn, but a stage always decides, so
      the self scope's bits answered for every vault. It now reads only the
      scope the vault falls under for the account (owner self; an admin-owned
      vault admin; otherwise user), through rbac::resolver::vaultScope, the
      function the admin vault resolver (admin.vaults.<scope>) and the
      sync/role globals resolver now share. FUSE's listing-override check reads
      the same single scope. Seeded presets are identical in every scope, so no
      seeded role changes; covered by VaultScopeRbacTest.
  * Development
    - Remove the dev-mode R2 test vault: the `dev.init_r2_test_vault` setting
      (config, ws `DevConfig`, console settings) and its seeding, which read
      the retired shared test-bucket credential, bound a vault to that bucket
      and cleared it at initdb. An existing `config.yaml` key is ignored.
  * Upstream events
    - R2 event subscriptions work against real Cloudflare: our rules are
      matched by the queue's id (dashes and case ignored) or name, since the
      bucket's notification configuration spells the queue id unlike the Queues
      API (every subscribe failed with "R2 accepted the event rule … but does
      not list it"); ensureChannel waits a few seconds for a queue it just
      created to answer its consumers lookup.
  * Lab tooling
    - Harden pentest fixture verification: exact daemon contract parity,
      disposable upload/overwrite/delete probes, content hashes, share
      isolation and inactive login checks; distinguish permission denials from
      probe errors.
    - Publish current verification and usage artifacts with stable checksums
      and secret checks; require restore evidence or an explicit data-loss
      waiver in the offline readiness gate.

 -- Cooper Larson <cooper@valkyrianlabs.com>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:46:05 +0000

vaulthalla (1.15.0-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * File recovery
    - Vault recovery preference (`off | local_snapshots | provider_versioning`,
      migration 111), shared recovery model and one authorizing boundary
      (`ops::recovery`), CLI `vh snapshot …`,
      `vh recovery list|inspect|plan|restore|delete|costs`,
      `vh vault recovery status|set|suspend|disclosure`, ws `recovery.*` /
      `vault.recovery.*`, web console Recovery history, restore preview, vault
      Recovery panel, cost disclosure dialog and recovery cost panel.
    - Local snapshots: explicit, named, encrypted recovery points of a file or
      directory tree (deduplicated zero-copy blobs under
      `<backing>/.vh-recovery/<vault>`), survive deletion of the live files
      (`file_versions.file_id` cascade replaced by SET NULL), count against a
      snapshot quota and the vault quota, restore to the original or an
      alternate destination with a CAS against concurrent writes.
    - Local versioning (opt-in): every content change keeps the prior sealed
      generation (keep-last / max-age / quota); in upstream vaults unsent
      generations form an ordered backlog replayed into a versioned bucket when
      the provider is reachable again; cache vaults release local bodies once
      the provider holds them.
    - Provider versioning (AWS S3): capability detection (R2 refused), explicit
      activation behind a signed cost disclosure, adoption of an existing
      vault's bucket, propagation window, noncurrent retention (30/90/365 days
      or indefinite; shortening needs its own acceptance), confirmed suspension
      that deletes nothing, a lifecycle rule expiring the index manifest's
      noncurrent versions, protected and unmanaged buckets never mutated.
    - Version-aware restore: provider copy (same key, ≤ 5 GiB) or bounded GET +
      re-seal + PUT under the sync pass lease; cache vaults adopt the restored
      generation index-only so nothing is uploaded twice; point-in-time
      directory plans are labelled timestamp-based reconstructions.
    - Local, telemetry-free usage accounting per vault
      (`vault_upstream_usage_daily`) and a decimal cost model for versioning
      (observed vs illustrative bases, ranges, retention scenarios, unpriced
      when no profile; never $0.00), cost alerts as operator notifications that
      never block writes.
    - Historical keys: retired vault key versions are unsealed on demand for
      recovery reads only and kept while recoverable data needs them (trigger
      `recovery_guard_retired_vault_key`); key export includes them.
    - Backing files are never written in place (new files are created
      exclusively or replaced by rename; contract test).
    - Re-sealing a retained generation wipes the decrypted (still
      unauthenticated) plaintext on every path, and unsealed retired keys leave
      memory once idle on a 30 s timer instead of waiting for the next recovery
      read.
    - Provider restore verifies what it restores: a plaintext version in a
      vault that encrypts upstream is refused (even when unverified versions
      are accepted) unless the vault's event subscription imports foreign
      objects, and a version Vaulthalla wrote must still match its recorded IV,
      key version and size.
    - Local versioning limits that delete retained versions sooner (a new or
      lower keep-last / max-age / quota, or turning versioning back on with
      limits) need the same explicit acceptance as a shorter provider
      retention, with a count of what the next retention run deletes; every
      recovery settings change is written to the audit log.
    - The versioning cost disclosure reveals the bucket's configuration (object
      lock, MFA delete, other applications' lifecycle rules) only to callers
      with admin.upstream.view and view on the vault's API key, like a status
      refresh; others get the cost figures without a provider request.
  * Fixes
    - fuse: rename over an existing file replaces it as rename(2) does
      (editors' temp-file saves failed with EIO; a failed cross-folder rename
      stranded the file's bytes); replacing needs Delete on the destination,
      and with local versioning on the replaced content is kept as a version
      (reason overwrite).
    - S3 data plane: the legacy signer puts the canonical query in SigV4 order
      (paged listings past 1,000 keys failed with SignatureDoesNotMatch on page
      2); a CompleteMultipartUpload 200 carrying an <Error> body is a failure.
    - fix(vault): a restart during an unfinished key rotation could not load
      the vault's key (`get_rotation_old_vault_key` selected `*` from
      `vault_keys_trashed`, which has no `updated_at`).
    - Deleting a folder (web console, ws `fs.entry.delete`) also drops its
      subfolders and symlinks from the filesystem metadata cache; recreating a
      path under the deleted folder failed.
    - S3 sync: a full bucket LIST throws when any page fails (transport error,
      403/503, cut or non-XML body) instead of returning the keys gathered so
      far, and every page of a listing reaches the remote index (buckets past
      1,000 objects indexed only their first page) (#246).
    - Upstream admin (AWS): HeadBucket goes straight to the bucket's known
      region (cached, `upstream_bucket.region`, else the key's region): one
      request instead of a global-endpoint probe plus a regional one; the
      global endpoint is probed only when no region is known or a redirect
      names none (#247).
    - S3 sync: a pass publishes the remote index manifest once (a vault's first
      pass PUT it twice: after the full LIST and again after its uploads); a
      pass that stops early still publishes the listed index; the expected 404
      for a missing manifest (and any absent object's HEAD) is logged at debug,
      not as an error (#248).
    - Local versioning: pending upstream versions of a deleted file whose key
      is never written again no longer stay pending forever: maintenance
      replays them into a bucket that keeps history (under the sync pass lease,
      price-preflighted, then deleted again so the file stays deleted) or marks
      them skipped (`orphaned`) when the bucket keeps none or never had the
      file, so retention can free their blobs; live and trashed keys are left
      to sync (#249).

